          "Let's see the declaration of the java.util.ArrayList class:",
          "Explanation:",
          "• public class ArrayList<E>:This declares the ArrayList class as a generic class. The type parameter <E> represents the type of elements stored in the ArrayList, allowing it to store any object type.\n• extends AbstractList<E>:This means ArrayList is a subclass of AbstractList. The AbstractList class provides a basic implementation of the List interface.\n• implements List<E>:This indicates that ArrayList implements the List interface, which defines methods for ordered collections.\n• implements RandomAccess:This is a marker interface that indicates ArrayList supports fast (constant-time) random access using an index.\n• implements Cloneable:This means ArrayList objects can be cloned using the clone() method.\n• implements Serializable:This allows ArrayList objects to be converted into a byte stream, so they can be saved to a file or transferred over a network."
