  "linear-search": {
    "title": "Linear Search in Java",
    "source": "tpointtech",
    "url": "https://www.tpointtech.com/linear-search-in-java",
    "sectionId": "java-collections-jdbc",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "",
        "paragraphs": [
          "Linear Search in JavaLast Updated : 31 Jan 2026Linear search is a technique that retrieves information from data structures. It is one of the simplest and most intuitive searching algorithms. It searches for a key element among multiple elements. It is not widely used because it is slower than binary search and hashing.Linear search is also known as sequential search. It is a straightforward method for finding an element within a list. It checks each element of the list sequentially until it finds a match or reaches the end of the list. The method is best suited for small or unsorted datasets where the overhead of more complex algorithms is not justified.Working of Linear SearchStarts from the first element.Compares each element with the target.Returns the index as soon as it finds a match.If it reaches the end without finding the target, it returns -1.AlgorithmLinearSearch(array, target)\n    for i from 0 to length(array) - 1\n        if array[i] == target\n            return i   // Target found at index i\n    return -1          // Target not found\nImplementation of Linear SearchIterative ApproachLet's see an example of linear search in Java, where we will search for an element sequentially in an array.Examplepublic class Main {\n\tpublic static int linearSearch(int[] arr, int target) {\n\t\tfor (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\n\t\t\tif (arr[i] == target) {\n\t\t\t\treturn i;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn -1;\n\t}\n\tpublic static void main(String a[]) {\n\t\tint[] a1 = { 10, 20, 30, 50, 70, 90 };\n\t\tint target = 50;\n\t\tSystem.out.println(target + \" is found at index: \" + linearSearch(a1, target));\n\t}\n}\nCompile and RunOutput:50 is found at index: 3ExplanationIn the above program, the linearSearch() method takes an array of integers and a target as parameters. The for loop iterates over the array. During each iteration, it compares the current array element with the target. If a match is found, it returns the index of the matching element. If the loop completes without finding the target, it returns -1.Recursive ApproachWe can also use a recursive approach to perform a linear search. In this approach, it calls itself for each index until the element is found or the array ends.Examplepublic class Main {\n\t// Recursive method to perform linear search\n\tpublic static int linearSearch(int[] array, int index, int target) {\n\t\t// compares the index value with the length of the array\n\t\tif (index >= array.length) {\n\t\t\treturn -1; // Base case: target not found\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (array[index] == target) {\n\t\t\treturn index; // Base case: target found\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn linearSearch(array, index + 1, target); // Recursive call\n\t}\n\tpublic static void main(String[] args) {\n\t\tint[] array = {5, 2, 8, 9, 3, 1};\n\t\tint target = 9;\n\t\tint result = linearSearch(array, 0, target);\n\t\tif (result != -1) {\n\t\t\tSystem.out.println(target + \" found at index \" + result);\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tSystem.out.println(target + \" not found in the array.\");\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\nCompile and RunOutput:9 is present at location 3ExplanationIn the above program, we have defined a method linearSearch() that takes three parameters: the array to be searched for, the index (from where to start the search), and the target element. First, we have checked the first base case, if the index value is greater than or equals to the length of the array, it returns the array. It means that the target element was not found.In the second base case, we have checked if the array index is equal to the target; if true, it returns the index. After that, we have recursively called the linearSearch() function. Every time when the function is called, the index value is incremented by 1.The recursive approach is less efficient in Java due to stack overhead, but it shows the power of recursion.Advantages and Disadvantages of Linear SearchAdvantagesSimplicity:It is easy to understand and implement.No Preprocessing:It does not require the data to be sorted.Versatility:It can be used on any type of data structure (arrays, linked lists, etc.).DisadvantagesInefficiency:On average, it requires checking half of the elements (O(n/2)) and, in the worst case, all elements (O(n)).Not Suitable for Large Datasets:It becomes impractical as the size of the dataset increases.Complexity AnalysisTime ComplexityBest Case: The best-case time complexity is O(1), which occurs when the target element is at the first position in the array.Worst Case: The time complexity of linear search is O(n), where n is the number of elements in the array. Because in the worst-case scenario, the algorithm has to check every element once.Space Complexity: The space complexity is O(1) because the algorithm uses a constant amount of additional space, regardless of the input size.Next TopicJava Programs ← prev next →"
        ],
        "code": []
      }
    ]
  },
